over a fixed neighbor list), so a specialized build","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"inlines it with no type erasure. Each read of a row calls it once: ","type":"text"},{"code":"successors(of:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":",","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"outEdges(of:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"code":"outDegree(of:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"code":"contains(edge:)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" once each, ","type":"text"},{"code":"edges","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"code":"edgeCount","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" once","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"per vertex. It must return the same sequence each time it is asked about the same vertex (the","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"protocol’s “asking again gives the same answer”); this is documented, not checked, and a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"closure that breaks it breaks every algorithm, as a mutating ","type":"text"},{"code":"==","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" breaks ","type":"text"},{"code":"Set","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":". Each target is","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"checked to be a vertex when it is read, not when the row is formed.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"On a hot path, return a small struct that computes the row up front into fixed slots and","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"yields them by count, rather than a ","type":"text"},{"code":"lazy.filter","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" over a fixed neighbor list: the filter calls","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"its predicate per element and keeps the unfiltered iterator’s state, and in the benchmarks on a","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"1000 × 1000 grid it cost 1.2–1.8× the precomputed row (breadth-first layers, components,","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text"